  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 3.3. Increased capacity of member States to formulate and implement policies for a just transition towards environmentally sustainable economies and societies</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Agencies under the Green Jobs Act compile available green jobs data and information with the purpose of establishing a monitoring system and performance indicators on green jobs, and to contribute to the adoption of an accepted definition by the Inter-Agency Committee on Labor, Income and Productivity Statistics and the Philippine Statistical Authority.

Sectoral/Thematic action plans developed and endorsed by tripartite constituents and other national partners in support of the Green Jobs Human Resource Development Plan implementation.  These serve as inputs for national plans and UN joint programming.

The country endorsed a Policy Note on the Green Jobs Act implementation that contributed to the Philippine position for the COP26, NDC commitments, and SDG performance. The ILO delivered the following outputs in the framework of the INSIGHT 2 project funded by the Government of Japan:

- Proposed methodology for the green jobs survey, including data collection, cleaning and analysis.
 
- Proposed plans for adoption to generate statistics and baseline data on Green Jobs.

- Proposed Green Jobs operational definition for adoption by the Interagency Committee on Green Jobs, including the Philippine Statistics Authority  Board.

- Proposed updates in the GJ Human Resource Development Plan for adoption and provision of strategic advice to the tripartite constituents.
 
- Policy note/s on the status of implementation of Green Jobs Act  and promotion of Green Skills &amp; Greening TVET in support of the National Green HRD Plan.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">3.3.1. Number of member States with policy measures to facilitate a just transition towards environmentally sustainable economies and societies through decent work</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 3.4. Increased capacity of ILO constituents to promote peaceful, stable and resilient societies through decent work</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Eleven communities benefit from completed water systems in the provinces of Lanao del Sur, Maguindanao and North Cotabato in the new Bangsamoro Autonomous Region in Muslim Mindanao (BARMM). These water systems  support 6,974 households, about 40,000 individuals.

The construction of water systems created 1,309 short-term jobs among community members, including indigenous peoples. 

Nine community contractors are now maintaining the new water facilities and ready to take on other labor-based construction initiatives. 

More than 2,000 short-term jobs were created under the Community Emergency Employment Programme to support those who have been affected by COVID-19.  The Ministry of Labor and Employment of BARMM has adopted CEEP as part of their regular programmes. The ILO delivered the following outputs in the framework of the WatSan project funded by the Government of Japan:

- Appropriate water supply development and sanitation subprojects with applied green works successfully established.
  
- Community contractors organized and trained on the construction, and proper operation and maintenance of the new water facilities in BARMM.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">3.4.1. Number of member States with programmes to promote peaceful, stable and resilient societies through decent work</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 3.1. Increased capacity of member States to formulate and implement a new generation of gender-responsive national employment policies, including for youth</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">On 25 June 2021, the President of the Philippines signed Executive Order No. 140, s. 2021 adopting the National Employment Recovery Strategy (NERS) 2021-2022 and reinforcing job-generating programs through the NERS Task Force. The NERS was endorsed by the social partners.

The NERS 2021-2022 is anchored on the International Labour Organizations&apos; four-pillar policy framework for responding and tackling the socio-economic impact of the COVID-19 crisis: (i) Stimulating the economy and employment; (ii) Supporting enterprises, jobs and incomes; (iii) Protecting workers in the workplace; and (iv) Relying on social dialogue for solutions. The NERS is in line with the ILO Employment Policy Convention, 1964 (No. 122) and its relevant recommendations. From March to May 2021, the ILO together with the World Bank and the Asian Development Bank, provided technical advisory services and operational support to the conduct of the National Job Summit activities together with government, workers&apos; and employers&apos; organizations toward the development of the National Employment Recovery Strategy (NERS). 

The ILO, with the support of the Government of Australia, provided technical and operational support in the completion of the assessments on the impact of technological changes on occupations and sectors and the multidimensional impact on employment and the labour market of COVID-19 in the Philippines. The assessments were presented to tripartite constituents for their validation and informed the NERS, UN Common Country Assessment (CCA), Socioeconomic and Peacebuilding Framework (SEPF) and other policies and measures on the future of work challenges, productive employment and skills development in the Philippines.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">3.1.1. Number of member States with new generation of national employment policies addressing country-specific future of work challenges</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 3.1. Increased capacity of member States to formulate and implement a new generation of gender-responsive national employment policies, including for youth</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The National Employment Recovery Strategy (NERS) 2021-2022 adopted in June 2021 includes a dedicated youth employability component as well as a set of other youth-relevant labour market measures as part of the 8-point employment recovery action plan. The ILO&apos;s assessment on the multidimensional impact on employment and the labour market of COVID-19 in the Philippines focuses on vulnerable groups and has a dedicated youth section to inform the NERS and other policies and measures on youth employment in the Philippines.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
